WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.612
Logical execution order explains many SQL rules that can otherwise feel arbitrary.

00:00:05.612 --> 00:00:10.977
PostgreSQL conceptually begins with FROM and JOIN, then applies WHERE, forms groups with GROUP BY,

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:17.247
filters groups with HAVING, evaluates SELECT expressions, performs window calculations,

00:00:17.247 --> 00:00:19.487
and then applies ORDER BY and LIMIT.

00:00:19.487 --> 00:00:25.807
This is why a same-level aggregate is not available in WHERE.

00:00:25.807 --> 00:00:28.742
The groups do not exist yet at that stage.

00:00:28.742 --> 00:00:33.232
HAVING can use aggregate expressions because grouping has already happened.

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:40.972
Window functions are evaluated after grouped filtering, which is why they can add analytical context to the

00:00:40.972 --> 00:00:45.307
rows that remain. You do not need to memorize every internal implementation detail.

00:00:45.307 --> 00:00:52.092
Use this logical order as a reasoning model for understanding which clause can see which values.

00:00:52.092 --> 00:00:57.022
Window functions calculate across related rows without collapsing them.

00:00:57.022 --> 00:01:03.627
That lets you keep row-level detail while adding rank, running totals, or group averages.

00:01:03.627 --> 00:01:10.777
Always define the partition and ordering carefully because those clauses describe which rows belong together

00:01:10.777 --> 00:01:12.119
and in what sequence.
